1
00:00:01,461 --> 00:00:13,904
(Q31) In today's class I'm going to talk about marine archaeology, the branch of archaeology focusing on human interaction with the sea, lakes and rivers.

2
00:00:13,904 --> 00:00:22,377
It's the study of ships, cargoes, shipping facilities, and other physical remains.

3
00:00:22,377 --> 00:00:32,372
I'll give you an example, then go on to show how this type of research is being transformed by the use of the latest technology.

4
00:00:32,372 --> 00:00:44,092
Atlit-Yam was a village on the coast of the eastern Mediterranean, which seems to have been thriving until around 7,000 BC.&nbsp;

5
00:00:44,092 --> 00:00:59,969
The residents kept cattle, caught fish and stored grain. They had wells for fresh water, many of their houses were built around a courtyard and were constructed of stone.

6
00:00:59,969 --> 00:01:13,907
[Q31答案句] The village contained an impressive monument: seven half-tonne stones standing in a semicircle around a spring, that might have been used for ceremonial purposes.

7
00:01:13,907 --> 00:01:28,326
(Q32) Atlit-Yam may have been destroyed swiftly by a tsunami, or climate change may have caused glaciers to melt and sea levels to rise, flooding the village gradually.&nbsp;

8
00:01:28,326 --> 00:01:38,311
Whatever the cause, it now lies ten metres below the surface of the Mediterranean, buried under sand at the bottom of the sea.

9
00:01:38,311 --> 00:01:47,628
It's been described as the largest and best preserved prehistoric settlement ever found on the seabed.

10
00:01:47,628 --> 00:01:52,756
For marine archaeologists, Atlit-Yam is a treasure trove.

11
00:01:52,756 --> 00:02:05,703
[Q32答案句] Research on the buildings, tools and the human remains has revealed how the bustling village once functioned, and even what diseases some of its residents suffered from.

12
00:02:05,703 --> 00:02:13,878
(Q33) But of course this is only one small village, one window into a lost world.

13
00:02:13,878 --> 00:02:22,652
For a fuller picture, researchers need more sunken settlements, but the hard part is finding them.

14
00:02:22,652 --> 00:02:45,821
Underwater research used to require divers to find shipwrecks or artefacts, but in the second half of the twentieth century, various types of underwater vehicles were developed, some controlled from a ship on the surface, and some of them autonomous, which means they don't need to be operated by a person.

15
00:02:45,821 --> 00:03:00,638
[Q33答案句] Autonomous underwater vehicles, or AUVs, are used in the oil industry, for instance, to create maps of the seabed before rigs and pipelines are installed.

16
00:03:00,638 --> 00:03:17,198
(Q34) [Q34答案句] To navigate they use sensors such as compasses and sonar. Until relatively recently they were very expensive, and so heavy that they had to be launched from a large vessel with a winch.

17
00:03:17,198 --> 00:03:32,806
(Q35) But the latest AUVs are much easier to manoeuvre - they can be launched from the shore or a small ship. And they're much cheaper, which makes them more accessible to research teams.

18
00:03:32,806 --> 00:03:48,853
They're also very sophisticated. They can communicate with each other and, for example, work out the most efficient way to survey a site, or to find particular objects on the seabed.

19
00:03:48,853 --> 00:03:52,657
Field tests show the approach can work.

20
00:03:52,657 --> 00:04:02,159
For example, in a trial in 2015, three AUVs searched for wrecks at Marzamemi, off the coast of Sicily.

21
00:04:02,159 --> 00:04:17,061
[Q35答案句] The site is the final resting place of an ancient Roman ship, which sank in the sixth century AD while ferrying prefabricated marble elements for the construction of an early church.

22
00:04:17,061 --> 00:04:25,319
(Q36) The AUVs mapped the area in detail, finding other ships carrying columns of the same material.&nbsp;

23
00:04:25,319 --> 00:04:33,128
Creating an internet in the sea for AUVs to communicate is no easy matter.

24
00:04:33,128 --> 00:04:43,105
Wifi networks on land use electromagnetic waves, but in water these will only travel a few centimetres.

25
00:04:43,105 --> 00:04:48,778
Instead, a more complex mix of technologies is required.

26
00:04:48,778 --> 00:04:59,801
[Q36答案句] For short distances, AUVs can share data using light while acoustic waves are used to communicate over long distances.

27
00:04:59,801 --> 00:05:18,987
(Q37) But more creative solutions are also being developed, where an AUV working on the seabed offloads data to a second AUV, which then surfaces and beams the data home to the research team using a satellite.

28
00:05:18,987 --> 00:05:27,979
There's also a system that enables AUVs to share information from seabed scans, and other data.

29
00:05:27,979 --> 00:05:51,050
[Q37答案句] So if an AUV surveying the seabed finds an intriguing object, it can share the coordinates of the object - that is, its position - with a nearby AUV that carries superior cameras, and arrange for that AUV to make a closer inspection of the object.

30
00:05:51,050 --> 00:05:58,698
(Q38) Marine archaeologists are excited about the huge potential of these AUVs for their discipline.

31
00:05:58,698 --> 00:06:06,606
One site where they're going to be deployed is the Gulf of Baratti, off the Italian coast.

32
00:06:06,606 --> 00:06:15,717
In 1974, a 2,000-year-old Roman vessel was discovered here, in 18 metres of water.&nbsp;

33
00:06:15,717 --> 00:06:24,372
[Q38答案句] When it sank, it was carrying medical goods, in wooden or tin receptacles.

34
00:06:24,372 --> 00:06:37,823
(Q39) [Q39答案句] Its cargo gives us insight into the treatments available all those years ago, including tablets that are thought to have been dissolved to form a cleansing liquid for the eyes.

35
00:06:37,823 --> 00:06:44,656
(Q40) Other Roman ships went down nearby, taking their cargoes with them.

36
00:06:44,656 --> 00:06:55,297
[Q40答案句] Some held huge pots made of terracotta. Some were used for transporting cargoes of olive oil, and others held wine.

37
00:06:55,297 --> 00:06:57,797
In many cases it's only these containers that remain, while the wooden ships have been buried under silt on the seabed.

